TRUE POSITIVES (4):
1. HTMLElementSanitizerConfiguration.isAllowUnknownProtocols() defaults to Boolean.TRUE - allows unknown URI protocols by default in the "secure" sanitizer
2. SecureHTMLElementSanitizer.isAllowedValue() - allowUnknownProtocols=true (default) bypasses the URI allowlist, only blocking *script: and data: patterns
3. SecureHTMLElementSanitizer.isAllowedDataValue() - allows any data: URI MIME type (including data:text/html) for img/audio/video/source elements without MIME type validation
4. InsecureHTMLElementSanitizer - allows all elements/attributes unconditionally; can be activated via execution context hint

FALSE POSITIVES (5):
1. LinkFilter noreferrer/noopener constants - these are defensive security additions, not vulnerabilities
2. LinkFilter.filter() contains() checks - idempotency checks for rel attribute values, not URL protocol checks
3. SecureHTMLElementSanitizer Pattern.compile without CASE_INSENSITIVE - patterns applied to already-lowercased input; critical patterns (IS_SCRIPT_OR_DATA, IS_ALLOWED_URI) correctly use CASE_INSENSITIVE
4. SanitizerFilter namespace string constants - defensive namespace confusion prevention (anti-mXSS)
5. SecureHTMLElementSanitizer contains()/toLowerCase()/matcher() checks - properly implemented allowlist checks; the real issue is allowUnknownProtocols default
